@node CMacs Brigade Undo
@chapter CMacs Brigade Undo: Undoing What an Agent Did

@cindex undo, agent actions
@cindex action log
@cindex cmacs-brigade-undo
@cindex action_undo
@cindex action_log

An agent edits a buffer, moves a window, switches your context, writes
a file.  @code{cmacs-brigade-undo} answers ``undo what the agent just
did''.

@table @kbd
@item M-x cmacs-brigade-undo
Undo the last undoable agent action; with a prefix @var{n}, the last
@var{n}, newest first.
@item M-x cmacs-brigade-undo-task
Everything one brigade run did.
@item M-x cmacs-brigade-undo-agent
Everything one agent did.
@item M-x cmacs-brigade-actions
The action log: inspect, undo, undo from here.
@end table

Every capability runs through one dispatch point,
@code{cmacs-brigade-call-tool} / @code{cmacs-brigade-call-tool-async},
whichever surface it arrived on: MCP, D-Bus forwarding,
@command{emacsctl tool}, an in-process agent, bacon.  That is where the
action log hooks in.  The code is in
@file{lisp/cmacs/cmacs-brigade-undo.el} (log, records, undo, commands),
@file{cmacs-brigade-undo-capture.el} (the per-tool capture functions),
@file{cmacs-brigade-undo-tools.el} (@code{action_log},
@code{action_undo}), with the dispatch in @file{cmacs-brigade-tools.el}
and the @code{:undo} key in @file{cmacs-brigade-registry.el}.

@menu
* Undo Recording::         Capture before, one change group, log after.
* Undo Undoing::           Staleness, order, force.
* Undo Log Buffer::        The *cmacs agent actions* buffer.
* Undo Tools::             action_log and action_undo; caller isolation.
* Undo Privacy::           Nothing on disk by default.
* Undo Writing::           Declaring an undo for your own tool.
* Undo Decisions::         Every destructive tool's decision.
* Undo Limits::            What it does not do.
@end menu

@node Undo Recording
@section How a Call Is Recorded

@subheading Before the handler: capture

A tool declares how it is undone with @code{:undo} on its
@code{cmacs-brigade-deftool} form.  Before the handler runs, the
dispatch asks that declaration for an @dfn{undo record}: plain data
about the state the call is about to change, plus the functions that
check and restore it.  Capturing before is the only general way: the
result of a call rarely says what was there before it.

@lisp
;; A capture function, named: called with the handler's arguments.
(cmacs-brigade-deftool set-buffer-content ...
  :destructive t
  :undo 'cmacs-brigade-undo-capture-buffer-text
  ...)

;; A FORM, evaluated with the parameters bound, before the body.
(cmacs-brigade-deftool my-rewrite "..." ((buffer string "Buffer"))
  :destructive t
  :undo (cmacs-brigade-undo-buffer-record buffer)
  (my/rewrite buffer))

;; A declared "cannot", with the reason the user will read.
(cmacs-brigade-deftool gowl-close-client ...
  :destructive t
  :undo '(irreversible "a closed application cannot be reopened as it was")
  ...)
@end lisp

The capture returns a record (a plist), @code{nil} (nothing to undo for
this call), or @code{irreversible} / @code{(irreversible @var{reason})}.
A capture that signals makes the call irreversible (``the state before
the call could not be captured'') and never fails the call: the log is
a record, not a gate.

A @code{:destructive} tool with an @code{:undo} is logged with its
record; one without is logged as ``irreversible: no undo declared''.  A
tool that is not destructive and declares no @code{:undo} is not logged
at all: reads are not actions.  A call that fails is not logged.

@subheading Around the handler: one change group

When the record names buffers, the dispatch wraps the handler in a
change group in each (@code{prepare-change-group} @dots{}
@code{accept-change-group} and @code{undo-amalgamate-change-group},
then an @code{undo-boundary}).  The agent's edit is then ONE entry in
that buffer's own undo list: a plain @kbd{C-/} in the buffer takes back
the whole edit, however many changes the handler made, and so does a
call that failed half way.

@subheading After the handler: complete and log

The record's @code{:after} function completes it with what the call
left: the changed region between two markers, the hash of the written
file, the geometry the window ended up with.  The call joins the
@dfn{action log}, an in-memory ring of
@code{cmacs-brigade-undo-log-size} entries (200), newest first.  An
entry has an id (@samp{#@var{id}}), a time, the caller (the dispatch's
agent string and the brigade task), the surface (@code{mcp},
@code{dbus}, @code{cli}, @code{agent}, @code{lisp}), the tool and its
arguments, the first line of the result, the record, and a state:
@code{done}, @code{undone}, @code{failed-to-undo}, @code{irreversible},
@code{stale} or @code{nothing-to-undo}.  An entry leaving the ring
releases what its record holds.

@node Undo Undoing
@section Undoing

@subheading Staleness

Before an inverse runs, the world must still be what the agent left:
for a buffer edit, the text between the markers is what the agent wrote
(the user may edit elsewhere meanwhile); for a file, its sha256 is that
of what the agent wrote; for the window layout, the frame's windows are
unchanged; for a gowl window, it exists and is where the agent left it
(or where it asked); focus, layout, workspace and context were not
changed again; a created buffer was not modified; point did not move.

Also, an older action refuses while a NEWER action that changed the same
object is still done (``#7 edit_buffer changed the same thing later;
undo it first'').  Newest first is always the order that works.

A refused undo says, in one sentence, what changed, and leaves the
entry @code{stale}, to be tried again once the world is back.  It never
clobbers the user's later work.  @code{cmacs-brigade-undo-force} (and
@kbd{F} in the log buffer) skips both checks after asking; it is for
the user only, and no tool passes it.

@subheading Buffer edits: the buffer's undo list first

The inverse of a buffer edit prefers the buffer's own undo list: when
the recorded change group is still intact at the head of
@code{buffer-undo-list}, it undoes exactly that group, as @kbd{C-/}
would.  Otherwise it replaces the region between the markers with the
text that was there.  A buffer over @code{cmacs-brigade-undo-max-bytes}
(1 MiB) is not snapshotted and can be undone only while the group is
intact.  The undo is itself one change group, so @kbd{C-/} in the
buffer re-applies the agent's edit: that is the only redo there is.
When the buffer was unmodified before and the undo brings back exactly
that text, it is unmodified again.

@subheading Order

@code{cmacs-brigade-undo} undoes the last @var{n} entries that have an
undo record, newest first, stopping at the first refusal and passing
over (and counting) irreversible ones.  @code{cmacs-brigade-undo-task},
@code{cmacs-brigade-undo-agent}, @kbd{U} in the log and
@code{action_undo} go newest first and stop at the first entry that
cannot be undone, saying which and why.

@node Undo Log Buffer
@section The Log Buffer

@kbd{M-x cmacs-brigade-actions} lists the log in
@samp{*cmacs agent actions*}, refreshed as entries arrive.

@table @kbd
@item RET
Inspect: caller, arguments, result, state and reason, what undo would
do.
@item u
Undo this entry.
@item U
Undo it and everything the same caller did after it.
@item F
Force-undo, after asking.
@item g
Refresh.
@end table

In the brigade dashboard, @code{cmacs-brigade-undo-dashboard-task}
undoes the task on the current line.

@node Undo Tools
@section The Tools

@table @code
@item action_log
Read-only.  The caller's OWN actions, newest first, one line each, with
what undoing would do.  @code{all=true} lists every caller's, only for
an agent name or task id in @code{cmacs-brigade-undo-log-all-callers}.
@item action_undo
Destructive, asks.  Undoes the caller's own actions only, by @code{id}
or the last @code{count}, with the staleness check always on.
@end table

Who the caller is comes from the dispatch
(@code{cmacs-brigade-current-call}), never from an argument.  A caller
with a task owns exactly that task's entries; the task id is the
editor's own record.  Without a task, entries with the same agent string
and no task: every external MCP client not on a scoped socket shares
the agent @code{mcp}, and so shares an identity.  Another caller's
@samp{#@var{id}} is answered exactly as one that does not exist.
Arguments, results and reasons in @code{action_log} go through
@code{cmacs-redact-text}; a missing redaction file fails the tool
closed.

@node Undo Privacy
@section Privacy and Disk

Records hold buffer and file contents, so the log is never written to
disk by default.

@table @code
@item cmacs-brigade-undo-log-size
Entries kept (200).
@item cmacs-brigade-undo-max-bytes
Largest buffer or file a record keeps a copy of (1 MiB).
@item cmacs-brigade-undo-backup-large-files
Non-nil keeps a backup copy of a larger file under
@file{$XDG_STATE_HOME/cmacs/undo/} (0700, files 0600), deleted when the
entry is undone or leaves the log; otherwise such a write is
irreversible with that reason.  Default nil.
@item cmacs-brigade-undo-persist-metadata
Non-nil appends id, time, tool, agent, task and state (never arguments,
results or records) to @file{$XDG_STATE_HOME/cmacs/undo/actions.jsonl}.
Default nil.
@item cmacs-brigade-undo-log-all-callers
Who may call @code{action_log} with @code{all=true}.
@end table

A file the agent created is undone by moving it to the trash, not by
deleting it.

@node Undo Writing
@section Writing an Undo for Your Own Tool

The record is a plist.  The machinery reads @code{:summary} (one line:
what undoing would do), @code{:object} (what the call changed, compared
with @code{equal}, for the newest-first rule), @code{:buffers} (buffers
to wrap in a change group), @code{:after} (@var{record} @var{result}
@result{} the completed record, @code{nil} or @code{(irreversible
@var{reason})}), @code{:stale} (@var{record} @result{} @code{nil} or a
sentence), @code{:inverse} (@var{record} @result{} undo; signal
@code{cmacs-brigade-undo-stale} when the world changed) and
@code{:cleanup}.  The building blocks are
@code{cmacs-brigade-undo-buffer-record}, @code{-created-buffer-record},
@code{-killed-buffer-record}, @code{-point-record},
@code{-file-record}, @code{-window-record} and
@code{cmacs-brigade-undo-compose}.  A capture function's docstring's
first line is what the decision table says about the tool.

@node Undo Decisions
@section Decisions for Every Destructive Tool

Every destructive shipped tool has a decision: an inverse,
@code{nothing} (it leaves no state behind), or irreversible with a
reason.  Tools whose files carry no @code{:undo} yet have theirs in
@code{cmacs-brigade-undo-decisions}.  The tests fail when a destructive
tool has none, and when this table is stale; regenerate it with
@code{cmacs-brigade-undo-batch-write-table}.

